title: 'stats on a stopped container: HTTP 200 carrying an error object neither check
  catches'
updated: 2026-08-27T17:20:57Z
---

Measured on Podman 5.4.2 (API 1.41), 2026-08-27.

GET /containers/{id}/stats on a container that exists but is not running is
answered with HTTP 200, Transfer-Encoding: chunked, and this body -- for both
stream=1 and the one-shot call:

  {"cause":"container is stopped","message":"container is stopped","response":500}

Neither guard in the transport sees it:
  - the >= 400 croak does not, because the status line is 200
  - _assert_no_stream_error does not, because its trigger is the errorDetail
    key alone and this object is keyed cause/message/response

So containers->stats($id) returns that HashRef where a reading was expected,
and with on_event the callback is handed it as though it were a reading.

Documented as a caveat in the stats POD (Containers.pm) for now; no code
change made. Open question for a decision, NOT to be fixed opportunistically:
whether the stream error check should also recognise Podman-shaped in-200
errors ({cause,message,response}), or whether that belongs in the transport as
a general "error object inside a 200" rule. It would be a public behaviour
change (a croak where callers now get a HashRef) and it touches Role::HTTP,
which is the seam under all twelve modules.

Related: the errorDetail default for stats itself was measured and kept loud
in this same round -- see the stats POD.
